## The permission

> If you modify this Program, or any covered work, by linking or
> combining it with a **Camera Support Library** (or a modified version
> of such a library), the licensors of this Program grant you additional
> permission to convey the resulting work. Corresponding Source for a
> non-source form of such a combination shall include the source code
> for the parts of this Program used in that combination, but need not
> include the source of the Camera Support Library itself.

A **Camera Support Library** means any library, driver, runtime or
software development kit whose purpose is to operate imaging hardware or
the equipment it is attached to, including but not limited to camera
manufacturers' SDKs, machine-vision and photographic-tethering
libraries, and motorised stage, filter-wheel and illumination control
libraries — together with any bindings distributed with them for the
purpose of using them.

This permission applies whether the library is linked, loaded at run
time, or invoked through a binding, and whether or not its own licence
is compatible with the GNU General Public License.

## Why it exists

A microscope camera is reachable only through its manufacturer's own
software, and that software is proprietary. Sometimes worse than
proprietary: the SDK darlaston was first written against ships with no
licence, no EULA and no copyright notice of any kind, so nobody can say
what it permits — downstream packagers have each invented a different
answer, and two of them contradict each other inside the same
repository.

Without this permission, the GPL's terms would make it doubtful whether
anyone could distribute a working build of darlaston at all, since every
useful build must reach a camera. That would not protect a single user's
freedom. It would only make the program undistributable while leaving
the proprietary SDK exactly as proprietary as it was.

So the permission is deliberately about a *class* of library rather than
one file. darlaston already reaches a dozen vendor libraries, and the
list is expected to grow to tethering and industrial-camera libraries.
Naming them one at a time would mean amending this document — and
therefore needing the agreement of every contributor — each time someone
adds support for a camera they own.

## What it does not do

It grants nothing about the hardware libraries themselves. Their own
licences, where they have any, are unaffected, and this document gives
you no right to redistribute them. darlaston does not bundle any of
them; you install your own. See `SUPPORT.md`.

It is also not a general exception. It covers libraries that exist to
operate imaging hardware. It does not cover linking darlaston with
proprietary software for any other purpose.

## Removing it

If you modify this Program, you may extend this permission to your
modified version, but you are not obliged to. If you do not wish to do
so, delete this file and the corresponding notice from the files you
modify.
